{"strategy": "always-cheapest", "item_id": "xmodpow-03", "category": "xmodpow", "difficulty": "xhard", "ts": 1791925261.351841, "latency_ms": 2863.8, "retries": 0, "http_status": 200, "status": "ok", "passed": true, "detail": "got 144, want 144", "model": "groq-gpt-oss-20b", "tier": "small", "attempts": 1, "prompt_tokens": 108, "completion_tokens": 2067, "shadow_cost_usd": 0.0006282, "response": "To find \\(423^{4906}\\pmod{1395}\\) we factor \\(1395\\) and use the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\n**1. Factor \\(1395\\).**  \n\\(1395 = 3^2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 31\\).\n\n**2. Compute the power modulo each prime power.**\n\n- Mod \\(9\\):  \n  \\(423 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}\\) (since \\(423 = 9\\cdot47\\)).  \n  Hence \\(423^{4906} \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}\\).\n\n- Mod \\(5\\):  \n  \\(423 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\\).  \n  \\(\\phi(5)=4\\), so \\(3^{4906}\\equiv 3^{4906\\bmod 4}=3^2\\equiv 9\\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n- Mod \\(31\\):  \n  \\(423 \\equiv 20 \\pmod{31}\\).  \n  \\(\\phi(31)=30\\), so \\(20^{4906}\\equiv 20^{4906\\bmod 30}=20^{16}\\pmod{31}\\).  \n  Compute:\n  \\[\n  20^2\\equiv 28,\\;\n  20^4\\equiv 28^2\\equiv 9,\\;\n  20^8\\equiv 9^2\\equiv 19,\\;\n  20^{16}\\equiv 19^2\\equiv 20 \\pmod{31}.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(423^{4906}\\equiv 20 \\pmod{31}\\).\n\n**3. Combine the congruences.**\n\nWe need \\(x\\) such that  \n\\[\nx \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9},\\quad\nx \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5},\\quad\nx \\equiv 20 \\pmod{31}.\n\\]\n\nFirst combine the first two:\n\\(x = 9k\\).  \n\\(9k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\Rightarrow 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\Rightarrow k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\).  \nSo \\(k = 1+5t\\) and \\(x = 9 + 45t\\).  \nThus \\(x \\equiv 9 \\pmod{45}\\).\n\nNow impose the third congruence:\n\\(9 + 45t \\equiv 20 \\pmod{31}\\).  \nSince \\(45 \\equiv 14 \\pmod{31}\\), we have  \n\\(9 + 14t \\equiv 20 \\pmod{31}\\Rightarrow 14t \\equiv 11 \\pmod{31}\\).  \nThe inverse of \\(14\\) mod \\(31\\) is \\(20\\) (because \\(14\\cdot20=280\\equiv1\\)).  \nThus \\(t \\equiv 20\\cdot11 \\equiv 220 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{31}\\).\n\nTake \\(t = 3\\).  \nThen \\(x = 9 + 45\\cdot3 = 144\\).\n\n**4. Verify.**  \n\\(144 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}\\),  \n\\(144 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\),  \n\\(144 \\equiv 20 \\pmod{31}\\).  \nAll conditions satisfied.\n\nHence the remainder when \\(423^{4906}\\) is divided by \\(1395\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{144}\n\\]", "response_truncated": false}
